By the reflection-generation theorem, an element of $SO(3)$ is either the identity or a product of two reflections in nonparallel planes. The intersection of the two planes is a line $l$ through the origin, and both reflections fix $l$ pointwise, so their composition fixes $l$ pointwise.

If a vector outside $l$ were fixed, the same comparison used for two-dimensional rotations would force the two normal vectors to be parallel. Therefore the transformation fixes exactly the line $l$ pointwise and is a rotation.
